# How a file a build writes comes into being, and the one rule that lets
# two builds in one tree share `build/` (design.md §3.1, §1.12; defect 134).
#
# THE RULE. A file another process may read is written under a name no
# other process uses, and only then published, by `rename_over`, under the
# name readers ask for. A reader therefore sees the whole of one version or
# the whole of another and never a file somebody is still writing, and a
# build killed half-way leaves only a private name nobody reads. A file
# only its writer reads (clang's stderr, a dependency listing, a
# pkg-config answer) needs the first half alone: a private name, removed
# once read.
#
# **Every one of those names used to be one name**, `object + ".tmp"`, a
# `clang-stderr.txt` per directory, one `build/pkg-config-out.txt` for the
# whole tree, and the C file written in place. Measured 2026-09-30 on this
# compiler's predecessor, six `heroes run` of one program started together
# on a cold `build/`: five of six failed, in each of five rounds, the first
# publish taking the one staged file the other five were about to publish.
#
# THE NAME is this process's id and a number no earlier call in this
# process has had (`process.serial`), so two processes never share one and
# neither does one process asking twice. It sits beside its destination,
# in the same directory, because a rename across two volumes is a copy.
#
# WHEN THE PUBLISH FAILS. POSIX `rename` replaces its destination
# atomically and never refuses because a reader holds it. Windows'
# `MoveFileEx` does: over a file another process has open, or a binary it
# is running, it answers 5 or 32. Where the destination is KEYED, named by
# a digest of everything its bytes depend on, a file another build put
# there IS this file, so it is kept and the staged copy thrown away. Where
# it is not, an author's `-o` naming a place rather than a content, nothing
# is assumed and the failure is reported with the path and the operating
# system's reason. Which destinations are keyed is the caller's to say,
# because only the caller knows what the name was derived from.
#
# THE AUTHOR'S OWN FILE (defect 136). A file whose name the author gave, the
# source `fmt --in-place` and `check --apply --in-place` rewrite and the C
# an `--emit-c -o` names, is published the same way and for the same reason
# (`rewrite`): measured 2026-09-30 before this, both in-place verbs wrote
# with `fopen(path, "wb")`, which empties the file first, and a disk 8 KB
# from full left 0 bytes of a 62,706-byte program. But a rename makes a NEW
# file, and the in-place write kept the old one with everything it was
# besides its bytes: its mode, owner, group, flags, ACL and extended
# attributes, a symbolic link to it, a second name (`runtime/parts/replace.c`
# has each measurement). So a link is followed to the file it names, a file
# that could not be opened for writing or that has a second name is refused,
# and the new file takes the old one's identity before it takes its name.
# What cannot be carried is refused, a new text that could not take its place
# is removed whatever it carries, and every refusal and failure says what
# failed and that the file is untouched.
#
# THE PRICE, measured the same day: a rename needs the whole new text on the
# disk beside the old one, so a disk with room for the new text but not for
# both now refuses where the in-place write fitted (`fmt` at 16 to 64 KB
# free, 80,708 bytes for 62,706); a directory the author may not write, and
# a file an ACL denies deleting, are refused where they were written in
# place; and a process killed between the stage and the rename leaves its
# private name beside the file, whole or cut, and the file as it was.

use cli/files
use cli/process

# A name for `path` that no other process, and no other call in this one,
# will ever ask for. The last part of the path keeps at most NAME_KEPT bytes
# of its own name, cut at a character, so that what is added never makes a
# name one part of a path cannot hold: a source named with 245 bytes and
# `.hero` took 264 with the suffix, past the 255 bytes APFS, ext4 and NTFS
# allow a part (the premise, and `private_name`'s case below is the test
# that fires if a part is ever held to fewer than 255 minus the suffix).
function private_name(path: str) -> str
    suffix = "." + process.pid().to_str() + "-" + process.serial().to_str() + ".tmp"
    cut: i64 @ path.len()

    while cut > 0 && path[cut - 1] != 47 && path[cut - 1] != 92
        cut @ cut - 1

    if path.len() - cut <= NAME_KEPT
        return path + suffix
    end: i64 @ cut + NAME_KEPT

    # Back to the first byte of a character: a UTF-8 continuation byte is
    # 0x80 to 0xBF, and a slice may not split one.
    while path[end] >= 128 && path[end] < 192
        end @ end - 1

    return path.slice(from: 0, to: end) + suffix

# How much of a name a private name keeps: 200 bytes, and the suffix is at
# most 44 (two numbers of at most 19 digits, `.`, `-`, `.tmp`), so a part is
# never over 244.
constant NAME_KEPT: i64
    200

# `text`, written whole under a private name, flushed to the device, and then
# published as `path`. **The flush is what a power cut asks for** (defect 357):
# a name that reached the disk ahead of its bytes was a file of zeros at its
# length after the Windows box's crash of 2026-10-04, read back by every later
# build as what clang said. `files.stage` writes, flushes and only then answers,
# as `runtime/parts/replace.c` does for the author's own files, and a failed
# stage leaves no file behind. **It never opens a name that is taken**, where
# `write_file` emptied it: a private name a killed build left behind is this
# process's own name only by a pid used again, so it is passed over for the
# next, a few times and no more.
function write_whole(path: str, text: str, keyed: bool) -> ()?
    staged: str @ private_name(path)
    made: ()? @ files.stage(staged: staged, text: text, like: "")
    tries: i64 @ 1

    while made.is_err() && process.path_exists(staged) && tries < STAGE_TRIES
        staged @ private_name(path)
        made @ files.stage(staged: staged, text: text, like: "")
        tries @ tries + 1

    if made.is_err()
        return fail(code: "not_written", msg: "cannot write " + path + message_of(made))
    return publish(staged: staged, path: path, keyed: keyed)

# How many private names one write asks for before it says it cannot write.
constant STAGE_TRIES: i64
    4

# Move a finished private file into place as `path`. The failure that is
# accepted is asked of the destination rather than of the reason: a keyed
# FILE is there, which is another build's copy of these bytes, whatever
# the platform's number for its refusal. A directory there is no artifact.
function publish(staged: str, path: str, keyed: bool) -> ()?
    if !process.rename_over(from: staged, to: path).is_err()
        return ok()
    why = process.because()
    discard(staged)

    if keyed && process.path_exists(path) && !process.is_directory(path)
        return ok()
    return fail(code: "not_written", msg: "cannot publish " + path + why)

# A private file this process is finished with. A removal that fails
# leaves a name nobody will ask for, which is why its answer is dropped.
function discard(path: str)
    _ = process.remove_file(path).is_err()

# `text` in place of the author's file at `path`, whole or not at all.
#
# Something at the landing that is not a file, `/dev/null` as an `-o`, holds
# no bytes a failed write could lose and cannot be renamed over, so it is
# written directly, as `fopen` wrote it until defect 134's publish refused
# it (measured: `--emit-c -o /dev/null` exit 2 since, where clang takes it).
function rewrite(path: str, text: str) -> ()?
    at = match landing_for(path)
        .ok found => found
        .err e    => return fail(code: e.code, msg: e.msg)

    match files.entry(at)
        .absent => return staged_over(path: path, at: at, text: text, like: "")
        .file   => return carried_over(path: path, at: at, text: text)
        .other =>
            if write_file(path: at, text: text).is_err()
                return fail(code: "not_written", msg: "cannot write " + path + process.because())
            return ok()
        .directory => return untouched(path: path, what: "a directory is there")
        .link      => return untouched(path: path, what: "it is still a link once its links are followed")
        .unknown   => return untouched(path: path, what: "it cannot be looked at" + process.because())

# Where a write to the author's `path` lands, an `-o` of any kind or the
# source an in-place verb rewrites: the file its links finally name.
# Refused, in `rewrite`'s words and before anything is written, where
# nothing may be written there: a chain of links that cannot be followed, a
# directory, a directory that is not there, which is the author's to make as
# the linker never made it, and a file that could not be opened for writing.
# A rename needs only its directory to be writable, so without that last
# question it replaces what the author protected: `--emit-c -o` and `build
# -o` both replaced a read-only file from defect 134's repair until this
# one, where clang's and ld's own `-o` refuse it (measured).
function landing_for(path: str) -> str?
    at = match files.landing(path)
        .ok found => found
        .err e    => return refused(path: path, what: "the link cannot be followed" + e.msg)

    match files.entry(at)
        .file =>
            open = files.writable(at)

            if open.is_err()
                return refused(path: path, what: "it cannot be opened for writing" + message_of(open))
            return ok(at)
        .directory => return refused(path: path, what: "a directory is there")
        .absent =>
            if beside(path: at, name: "").len() > 0 && !process.is_directory(beside(path: at, name: "."))
                return refused(path: path, what: "its directory is not there")
            return ok(at)
        .link | .other | .unknown => return ok(at)

# `untouched`'s sentence, as the failure of a question that answers a path.
function refused(path: str, what: str) -> str?
    return fail(code: "not_written", msg: message_of(untouched(path: path, what: what)))

# The last part of a path, and a sibling of it: `-o same` has no directory
# at all, and a sibling of it is a name in the working directory. Cut at `/`
# alone, which every platform takes: `\` is Windows' separator and a byte of
# a name elsewhere, so a path is never split at it.
function base_of(path: str) -> str
    at: i64 @ path.len()

    while at > 0 && path[at - 1] != 47
        at @ at - 1

    return path.slice(from: at, to: path.len())

function beside(path: str, name: str) -> str
    return path.slice(from: 0, to: path.len() - base_of(path).len()) + name

# The author's existing file, replaced by one that is everything it was but
# its bytes, or refused before a byte is written.
function carried_over(path: str, at: str, text: str) -> ()?
    names = files.names_of(at)

    if names < 1
        return untouched(path: path, what: "its names cannot be counted" + process.because())

    if names > 1
        return untouched(
            path: path,
            what: "it has " + names.to_str() + " names, and a new file under this one would leave the others holding the old text"
        )
    return staged_over(path: path, at: at, text: text, like: at)

# The new text under a private name beside `at`, then renamed over it, `at`
# being the landing of the author's `path`.
function staged_over(path: str, at: str, text: str, like: str) -> ()?
    return staged_through(path: path, at: at, staged: private_name(at), text: text, like: like)

# The same through a private name the caller chose, which is how the cases
# below make a stage and a rename fail on every platform. A stage that fails
# leaves no file; a rename that fails leaves the staged one, removed here
# whatever ACL it carried over (`process.unstage`).
function staged_through(path: str, at: str, staged: str, text: str, like: str) -> ()?
    made = files.stage(staged: staged, text: text, like: like)

    if made.is_err()
        return untouched(path: path, what: stage_words(made) + message_of(made))
    moved = files.replace(staged: staged, path: at)

    if moved.is_err()
        what = "its new text could not be put in its place" + message_of(moved)

        if files.unstage(staged).is_err()
            return untouched(path: path, what: what + ", and it is left in " + staged)
        return untouched(path: path, what: what)
    return ok()

# What a failed stage could not do, by the step `process.stage` names.
function stage_words(failed: ()?) -> str
    step = match failed
        .ok    => ""
        .err e => e.code

    match step
        "create"     => return "no file could be made beside it for its new text"
        "write"      => return "its new text could not be written whole"
        "owner"      => return "its new text could not be given its owner and group"
        "mode"       => return "its new text could not be given its permissions"
        "flags"      => return "its new text could not be given its flags"
        "attributes" => return "its new text could not be given its ACL and extended attributes"
        "flush"      => return "its new text could not be flushed to the disk"
        _            => return "its new text could not be written, at " + step

# The one sentence every refusal and failure of `rewrite` ends in: the file
# as it was, or, where nothing was there, still nothing. Asked of the path
# when the sentence is written, because that is the moment it describes.
function untouched(path: str, what: str) -> ()?
    ending: str @ path + " is untouched"

    if nothing_at(path)
        ending @ path + " was not created"
    return fail(code: "not_written", msg: "cannot write " + path + ": " + what + "; " + ending)

function nothing_at(path: str) -> bool
    match files.entry(path)
        .absent                                        => return true
        .file | .directory | .link | .other | .unknown => return false

# A failure's message, "" for none: what the verbs print after `error: `.
function message_of(failed: ()?) -> str
    match failed
        .ok    => return ""
        .err e => return e.msg

## Tests

function holds(text: str, needle: str) -> bool
    if needle.len() > text.len()
        return false
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at + needle.len() <= text.len()
        if text.slice(from: at, to: at + needle.len()) == needle
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

function failure_of(failed: ()?) -> str
    match failed
        .ok    => return ""
        .err e => return e.code + ": " + e.msg

test "a private name is this process's own, and a second ask is another name"
    one = private_name("build/x.o")
    two = private_name("build/x.o")
    assert one != two
    assert holds(text: one, needle: "build/x.o." + process.pid().to_str() + "-")
    assert holds(text: one, needle: ".tmp")

test "a publish replaces what was there with the whole of what was written, and leaves no private name"
    dir = "build/selftest-publish"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/target", text: "OLD").is_err()
    assert !write_whole(path: dir + "/target", text: "NEW", keyed: false).is_err()
    assert read_file(dir + "/target").must() == "NEW"
    assert process.files_under(dir).must() == [dir + "/target"]

# Defect 357's repair stages through `files.stage`, which never opens a name
# that is taken: the name a killed build left, met again under a pid used
# again, is passed over and left as it was, and the write lands whole.
test "a private name a killed build left behind is passed over and never written into (defect 357)"
    dir = "build/selftest-publish-stale"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    target = dir + "/kept.txt"
    asked = private_name(target)
    serial: i64 @ 0
    at: i64 @ (target + "." + process.pid().to_str() + "-").len()

    while at < asked.len() - 4
        serial @ serial * 10 + (asked[at] - '0').to_i64().must()
        at @ at + 1

    left = target + "." + process.pid().to_str() + "-" + (serial + 1).to_str() + ".tmp"
    assert !write_file(path: left, text: "LEFT BY A KILLED BUILD").is_err()
    assert !write_whole(path: target, text: "NEW", keyed: true).is_err()
    assert read_file(target).must() == "NEW"
    assert read_file(left).must() == "LEFT BY A KILLED BUILD"

# A rename that fails on every platform with a file already at the
# destination: a staged name nothing was written to. The destination is the
# other build's copy. **This case first made the staged name a DIRECTORY,
# which this comment said nothing renames over a file on any platform; the
# Windows box renamed it** (2026-09-30, `MoveFileExA` with
# `MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING` put the directory where the file was, where
# POSIX answers ENOTDIR). Nothing a build publishes is a directory, so the
# case changed and not the rule.
test "a keyed destination another build published is kept"
    dir = "build/selftest-publish-keyed"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/artifact", text: "THEIRS").is_err()
    staged = private_name(dir + "/artifact")
    assert !publish(staged: staged, path: dir + "/artifact", keyed: true).is_err()
    assert read_file(dir + "/artifact").must() == "THEIRS"

test "an author's destination is never kept that way: the failure names it and says why"
    dir = "build/selftest-publish-author"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/app", text: "EARLIER").is_err()
    staged = private_name(dir + "/app")
    said = failure_of(publish(staged: staged, path: dir + "/app", keyed: false))
    assert holds(
        text: said,
        needle: "not_written: cannot publish " + dir + "/app: the operating system's own reason is "
    )
    assert read_file(dir + "/app").must() == "EARLIER"

# A rename that fails on both platforms with the staged file written: a file
# over a directory. So this is also the case where the staged copy is there
# to be thrown away, and is.
test "a directory where a keyed file belongs is no artifact, and is reported, and the staged copy is thrown away"
    dir = "build/selftest-publish-dir"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir + "/artifact").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/artifact/inside", text: "x").is_err()
    said = failure_of(write_whole(path: dir + "/artifact", text: "MINE", keyed: true))
    assert holds(text: said, needle: "cannot publish " + dir + "/artifact")
    assert process.files_under(dir).must() == [dir + "/artifact/inside"]

test "a write that cannot happen names the path and the operating system's reason"
    said = failure_of(write_whole(path: "build/selftest-publish-none/deeper/x.c", text: "int x;", keyed: true))
    assert holds(
        text: said,
        needle: "not_written: cannot write build/selftest-publish-none/deeper/x.c: the operating system's own reason is "
    )

# DEFECT 136's CASES, one per rule `rewrite` keeps. Each builds its shape
# under `build/`; the modes, the second name and the symbolic link are made
# through the runtime, the one place that can make them on every platform.

# A directory emptied for a case, its files made writable and removed one by
# one first: Windows will not remove a read-only file, Darwin will not remove
# one whose ACL denies it (`process.unstage` clears that), and a case that
# failed half-way leaves one behind for the next run.
function fresh(dir: str)
    if process.is_directory(dir)
        for file in process.files_under(dir).default([])
            _ = files.set_mode(path: file, mode: 0o644).is_err()
            _ = files.unstage(file).is_err()
    _ = process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    _ = process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()

function is_link(path: str) -> bool
    match files.entry(path)
        .link                                            => return true
        .absent | .file | .directory | .other | .unknown => return false

test "a rewrite puts the whole new text in the author's file, makes an absent one, and leaves nothing beside either"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
    assert !rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW").is_err()
    assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must() == "NEW"
    assert !rewrite(path: dir + "/out.c", text: "int x;\n").is_err()
    assert read_file(dir + "/out.c").must() == "int x;\n"
    assert process.files_under(dir).must().len() == 2

test "a new text that cannot be written leaves the author's file byte for byte, and says what failed and that it is untouched"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-unwritten"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "THE AUTHOR'S TEXT").is_err()

    # The private name is taken, by a directory, so the new file cannot be
    # made; and the directory is not removed, because it was not this
    # process's to remove.
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir + "/taken").is_err()
    said = failure_of(
        staged_through(path: dir + "/p.hero", at: dir + "/p.hero", staged: dir + "/taken", text: "NEW", like: dir + "/p.hero")
    )
    assert holds(
        text: said,
        needle: "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/p.hero: no file could be made beside it for its new text: the operating system's own reason is "
    )
    assert holds(text: said, needle: "; " + dir + "/p.hero is untouched")
    assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must() == "THE AUTHOR'S TEXT"
    assert process.is_directory(dir + "/taken")

# A file renamed over a directory that holds one fails on every platform
# (the keyed case above), after the stage has written its whole copy.
test "a new text that cannot take the file's place leaves it as it was, and its copy is removed"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-unplaced"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir + "/p.hero").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero/inside", text: "KEPT").is_err()
    said = failure_of(
        staged_through(path: dir + "/p.hero", at: dir + "/p.hero", staged: dir + "/staged", text: "NEW", like: "")
    )
    assert holds(
        text: said,
        needle: ": its new text could not be put in its place: the operating system's own reason is "
    )
    assert holds(text: said, needle: "; " + dir + "/p.hero is untouched")
    assert process.files_under(dir).must() == [dir + "/p.hero/inside"]
    assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero/inside").must() == "KEPT"

# The world is asked by the route the repair replaced: `fopen` refuses a
# read-only file to anybody but a superuser, and the Linux container the
# batch gate runs in is one, where root opens it and the rewrite must too.
test "a read-only file is refused as fopen refused it and left byte for byte, and keeps its mode either way"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-readonly"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/probe", text: "x").is_err()
    assert !files.set_mode(path: dir + "/p.hero", mode: 0o444).is_err()
    assert !files.set_mode(path: dir + "/probe", mode: 0o444).is_err()
    fopen_refuses = write_file(path: dir + "/probe", text: "y").is_err()
    said = failure_of(rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW"))
    kept = read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must()
    mode = files.mode_of(dir + "/p.hero")
    fresh(dir)
    assert mode == 0o444

    if fopen_refuses
        assert holds(
            text: said,
            needle: "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/p.hero: it cannot be opened for writing: the operating system's own reason is "
        )
        assert holds(text: said, needle: "; " + dir + "/p.hero is untouched")
        assert kept == "OLD"
    else
        assert said == ""
        assert kept == "NEW"

# 0600, 0640 and 0755, each other than what a file made here gets, 0644 under
# the usual mask; Windows keeps read-only alone, so there the two sides agree
# at 0666 and the case holds the same rule with less to see.
test "a file's permission bits are carried to its new text"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-mode"
    fresh(dir)

    for mode in [0o600, 0o640, 0o755]
        assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
        assert !files.set_mode(path: dir + "/p.hero", mode: mode).is_err()
        before = files.mode_of(dir + "/p.hero")
        assert !rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW").is_err()
        assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must() == "NEW"
        assert files.mode_of(dir + "/p.hero") == before

# `nodump` on Darwin and Linux, `hidden` on Windows: measured on the first
# two, the in-place write kept the flag and this repair's first version lost
# it. A filesystem that keeps no flags gives the case nothing to hold.
test "a file's flags are carried to its new text"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-flag"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()

    if files.set_flag(dir + "/p.hero").must()
        assert files.flagged(dir + "/p.hero") == 1
        assert !rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW").is_err()
        assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must() == "NEW"
        assert files.flagged(dir + "/p.hero") == 1

# Measured on Darwin: an ACL entry denying the file's deletion refuses the
# rename over it, and the staged copy, which carries the same entry, refused
# its own removal and stayed beside the file. Where no such entry exists the
# rewrite simply happens.
test "a new text that cannot take the place of a file its ACL protects is removed all the same"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-denied"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
    denied = files.deny_delete(dir + "/p.hero").must()
    said = failure_of(rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW"))
    left = process.files_under(dir).must()
    kept = read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must()
    fresh(dir)

    if denied
        assert holds(
            text: said,
            needle: "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/p.hero: its new text could not be put in its place: the operating system's own reason is "
        )
        assert holds(text: said, needle: "; " + dir + "/p.hero is untouched")
        assert !holds(text: said, needle: "left in")
        assert left == [dir + "/p.hero"]
        assert kept == "OLD"
    else
        assert said == ""
        assert kept == "NEW"

test "a file whose directory is not there is refused before anything is made"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-nowhere"
    fresh(dir)
    said = failure_of(rewrite(path: dir + "/deeper/out.c", text: "int x;\n"))
    assert said == "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/deeper/out.c: its directory is not there; " + dir + "/deeper/out.c was not created"
    assert !process.is_directory(dir + "/deeper")
    assert process.files_under(dir).must().len() == 0

test "a file with a second name is refused, and both names keep the old text"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-names"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
    assert !files.second_name(existing: dir + "/p.hero", name: dir + "/other.hero").is_err()
    said = failure_of(rewrite(path: dir + "/p.hero", text: "NEW"))
    assert said == "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/p.hero: it has 2 names, and a new file under this one would leave the others holding the old text; " + dir + "/p.hero is untouched"
    assert read_file(dir + "/p.hero").must() == "OLD"
    assert read_file(dir + "/other.hero").must() == "OLD"

# A chain of two links, each read from its own directory, all three names in
# one so that no separator is in a target. Windows makes a link for an
# administrator or in developer mode, which the box and CI's runner are;
# anywhere it may not, the fixture's own assert is this case's loud failure.
test "a symbolic link is written through to the file it names, and stays a link"
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-link"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !write_file(path: dir + "/t.hero", text: "OLD").is_err()
    assert !files.symbolic_link(target: "t.hero", name: dir + "/p.hero").is_err()
    assert !files.symbolic_link(target: "p.hero", name: dir + "/chain.hero").is_err()
    assert !rewrite(path: dir + "/chain.hero", text: "NEW").is_err()
    assert is_link(dir + "/chain.hero")
    assert is_link(dir + "/p.hero")
    assert read_file(dir + "/t.hero").must() == "NEW"

    for file in process.files_under(dir).must()
        assert !holds(text: file, needle: ".tmp")

test "what is not a file is written into, and a directory is refused untouched"
    assert !rewrite(path: process.discard(), text: "into nothing").is_err()
    dir = "build/selftest-rewrite-directory"
    fresh(dir)
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir + "/out.c").is_err()
    said = failure_of(rewrite(path: dir + "/out.c", text: "x"))
    assert said == "not_written: cannot write " + dir + "/out.c: a directory is there; " + dir + "/out.c is untouched"

# The premise `private_name` rests on, as the test that fires when it dies:
# one part of a path holds 255 bytes on every filesystem this writes to.
test "a private name keeps one part of a path within 244 bytes, cut at a character, and a short name whole"
    named = private_name("build/" + "n".repeat(250) + ".hero")
    last = named.slice(from: 6, to: named.len())
    assert last.len() <= 244
    assert holds(text: named, needle: "build/" + "n".repeat(200) + ".")
    wide = private_name("build/" + "é".repeat(150))
    assert wide.slice(from: 6, to: 206) == "é".repeat(100)
    assert holds(text: private_name("build/x.o"), needle: "build/x.o." + process.pid().to_str() + "-")
